WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.868
Gastric ultrasound, from start to finish.

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:05.638
For adults, use a curved, low-frequency probe.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:10.755
A linear, high-frequency probe suits children under forty kilograms, and slim adults.

00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:15.882
Choose the abdominal preset, and start with the patient supine or semi-recumbent.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.511
Place the probe in the epigastrium, in the sagittal plane, with the marker towards the head.

00:00:22.511 --> 00:00:28.650
Slide across until you see the edge of the liver and, deep to it, the aorta or the inferior vena cava.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.272
Here is the same slice in colour. The antrum lies just under the liver edge.

00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:38.974
Behind it are the pancreas, the superior mesenteric vessels and the aorta.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.197
Lying supine, this antrum looks empty: a small target with a thick, dark wall.

00:00:45.197 --> 00:00:49.483
But fluid may simply be sitting higher up, in the body and the fundus.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.632
So turn the patient onto the right side.

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:58.187
Fluid runs down into the antrum, which is why the right lateral position decides the grade.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.937
Same plane, new picture. The antrum now holds clear fluid:

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:04.881
black contents inside the wall.

00:01:04.881 --> 00:01:08.830
Fluid seen only in the right lateral position is grade one.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.585
The antrum contracts from time to time. Wait until it relaxes, then freeze the image.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.548
The machine gives you three ways to measure the area. Distance:

00:01:20.548 --> 00:01:23.889
two calipers at right angles, from serosa to serosa.

00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:27.801
The area is pi, times the two diameters, divided by four.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.163
Ellipse:

00:01:29.163 --> 00:01:33.911
stretch it along the long diameter, then widen it until it sits on the outer wall.

00:01:33.911 --> 00:01:36.246
The machine gives the area directly.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.221
Or trace:

00:01:38.221 --> 00:01:43.898
draw round the serosa, the outer bright line of the wall, and the machine reports the area inside.

00:01:43.898 --> 00:01:47.102
Whichever you use, take the mean of three readings.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:55.040
Take that area, with the patient's age, to the Perlas nomogram, or use the volume calculator on the NORA² website.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.818
Read off the volume, then divide by the weight.

00:01:57.818 --> 00:02:01.734
Here it is well below one point five millilitres per kilogram.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.707
Learn the other patterns too.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:09.507
An empty antrum, flat or a small target in both positions, is grade zero.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.898
Clear fluid in both positions is grade two. A larger volume is likely, so measure it.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.246
Thick fluid, such as milk, fills the antrum with even, bright echoes.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:29.017
Just after a meal, air mixed with the food gives a bright, frosted-glass front edge that hides the back wall.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.370
Later, solid food looks mixed and patchy.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.294
Thick fluid or solid means a full stomach, whatever the area.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:37.475
So:

00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:42.796
an empty antrum, or clear fluid up to one point five millilitres per kilogram, is low risk.

00:02:42.796 --> 00:02:46.787
More than that, or any solid, and you treat the stomach as full.
